`hllc` refuses to overwrite a `docker-compose.yml` it did not generate.
Co-located mode discovers output paths by scanning rather than being
told them, so the natural incremental migration — convert one service
to `.hll` while the rest stay hand-written — would otherwise destroy
every hand-tuned file in the tree on the first build.

```console
$ hllc --build web.hll --out docker-compose.yml
? 1
docker-compose.yml: refusing to overwrite a file hllc didn't generate (it has no `# Generated by hllc` header) — move it aside, or pass --force to overwrite it

```

`--force` is the way through, and the message says so rather than
leaving the user to find the flag.

```console
$ hllc --build web.hll --out docker-compose.yml --force
docker-compose.yml

```

Once the file carries the generated header, it is `hllc`'s own previous
output and is replaced without `--force` — otherwise every rebuild
after the first would need the flag, which would make it meaningless.

```console
$ hllc --build web.hll --out docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.yml

```
